# Line-ending policy.
#
# Without this file, a Windows clone with core.autocrlf=true (Git for Windows'
# default) checks every shell script out with CRLF. Docker then copies those
# scripts into the image verbatim, and the kernel tries to exec the interpreter
# named on the shebang line -- including the trailing carriage return. The
# result is a crash loop whose only message is:
#
#     exec /start-services.sh: no such file or directory
#
# which names a file that is demonstrably present. Both Dockerfile and
# Dockerfile.cloud install scripts/start_vllm.sh as their entrypoint, so on a
# Windows host neither image can boot without this.
#
# Git already stores these files with LF; this only pins how they are checked
# out, so adding it changes no file's content.

# Anything executed by a shebang inside a container must stay LF.
*.sh            text eol=lf

# Sourced by scripts/smoke_local_guardrails.sh. CRLF here would put a trailing
# carriage return on every value, so a port becomes "8000\r" and the URL built
# from it silently fails to connect.
.env*           text eol=lf

# Read by tooling inside containers, where CRLF is at best noise.
Dockerfile*     text eol=lf
*.yml           text eol=lf
*.yaml          text eol=lf
